    Section(
      title: 'Physical Examination Findings:',
      content:
          '**Vitals**: BP 94/58 (supine), HR 56, RR 14 (ventilator), Temp 37.1C, SpO2 98% on FiO2 0.30\n\n**General**: Alert young male in hospital bed, ventilator-dependent via tracheostomy. Communicates by mouthing words. Appears fatigued.\n\n**ASIA Examination**:\n- **Motor**: C5-T1 bilateral: 0/5; L2-S1 bilateral: 0/5. Right trapezius 2/5 (C3-C4 partial innervation). Left trapezius 0/5. Total motor score: 0/100.\n- **Sensory**: Light touch and pin prick intact C2-C3 bilaterally; absent C4 and below bilaterally (C4 light touch 1/2 on right only).\n- **Sacral Sparing**: None (VAC absent, DAP absent, S4-S5 absent).\n- **NLI**: C3\n- **AIS**: A (Complete)\n\n**Respiratory Examination**:\n- **Chest wall**: No visible respiratory excursion with ventilator disconnection (brief trial)\n- **Diaphragm**: No palpable or visible diaphragmatic excursion\n- **Cough**: Absent voluntary cough; requires suctioning for secretion management\n- **Lung auscultation**: Diminished bases bilaterally; scattered rhonchi clearing with suctioning; no wheezing or crackles\n- **Chest X-ray**: Low lung volumes, mild bibasilar atelectasis, no consolidation\n\n**Phrenic Nerve Assessment**:\n- **Diaphragm fluoroscopy (sniff test)**: No diaphragmatic excursion bilaterally (paradoxical movement noted -- negative sniff test)\n- **Phrenic nerve conduction study**: Bilateral phrenic nerve latency absent (no compound motor action potential recorded from either hemidiaphragm)\n- Note: Phrenic nerve studies at 3 weeks may reflect neurapraxia vs. axonotmesis; repeat at 3-6 months to reassess\n\n**Autonomic**: Resting HR 56 (loss of sympathetic cardiac innervation); BP 94/58 supine requiring midodrine 10 mg TID; poikilothermy present.\n**Skin**: Intact. Bilateral heel offloading boots in place. Sacrum intact on pressure redistribution mattress.\n**Tone**: Flaccid throughout (spinal shock).',
