    Section(
      title: '+ Explain the respiratory physiology of high cervical SCI and evaluate this patient\'s candidacy for diaphragm pacing.',
      content:
          '**Respiratory Physiology in High Cervical SCI**:\n\n**Key Respiratory Muscles and Innervation**:\n- **Diaphragm (C3-C5)**: Primary muscle of inspiration, responsible for ~65-75% of resting tidal volume. In C3 injury, bilateral diaphragm paralysis occurs -- this is why the patient is ventilator-dependent.\n- **Accessory muscles**: Sternocleidomastoid (CN XI/C2-C3) and upper trapezius (CN XI/C3-C4) -- may be partially preserved in this patient (right trapezius 2/5). These provide minimal tidal volume (~200-300 mL) and cannot sustain ventilation.\n- **Intercostals (T1-T11)**: Paralyzed. Loss of chest wall expansion and stabilization.\n- **Abdominals (T6-T12)**: Paralyzed. Loss of forced expiration and effective cough.\n- **Scalenes (C3-C8)**: Paralyzed in C3 injury.\n\n**Consequences**:\n- Complete loss of voluntary ventilation\n- Ineffective cough (expiratory muscles paralyzed) leading to secretion retention, atelectasis, pneumonia\n- Reduced lung compliance over time (chest wall stiffness from immobility)\n- V/Q mismatch from dependent atelectasis\n- **Pneumonia is the #1 cause of death** in chronic high cervical SCI\n\n**Diaphragm Pacing Candidacy**:\n\nDiaphragm pacing (also called phrenic nerve pacing) uses implanted electrodes to electrically stimulate the phrenic nerves, causing diaphragm contraction and inspiration. This can allow ventilator-free breathing in selected patients.\n\n**Candidacy Criteria**:\n1. **Intact lower motor neurons**: The phrenic nerve anterior horn cells (C3-C5) must be intact. This is the case in **upper motor neuron** lesions (injury above C3) or injuries where the phrenic nerve cell bodies are spared.\n2. **Phrenic nerve conduction**: Must demonstrate an intact phrenic nerve-to-diaphragm pathway. **This patient currently has absent phrenic nerve responses at 3 weeks -- but this is TOO EARLY to determine candidacy.** Early absence may reflect:\n   - **Neurapraxia**: Temporary conduction block from edema/contusion at the C3 level; may recover as swelling resolves\n   - **Axonotmesis/Neurotmesis**: Axonal damage at the anterior horn cell level; will NOT recover\n3. **Repeat phrenic nerve studies at 3-6 months** post-injury to allow time for neurapraxia to resolve and Wallerian degeneration to complete\n4. **If phrenic nerve is intact on repeat testing**: Patient IS a candidate for phrenic nerve pacing\n5. **If phrenic nerve remains absent**: Patient is NOT a candidate for standard phrenic pacing; may be considered for:\n   - **Intercostal-to-phrenic nerve transfer** (emerging surgical technique)\n   - **Direct diaphragm pacing** (intramuscular electrodes placed laparoscopically into the diaphragm -- NeuRx system)\n\n**Benefits of Diaphragm Pacing**:\n- Ventilator-free breathing for hours per day (some patients achieve 24-hour independence)\n- Improved speech quality (airflow-driven phonation)\n- Enhanced smell and taste\n- Reduced respiratory infections\n- Greater mobility (portable, no ventilator circuit)\n- Improved quality of life and independence\n- Reduced caregiver burden',
    ),
    Section(
      title: 'DOMAIN C: PATIENT MANAGEMENT',
