    Section(
      title: '+ Describe your ventilator weaning protocol and pulmonary management plan for this patient.',
      content:
          '**Ventilator Weaning Protocol for High Cervical SCI**:\n\n**1. Progressive Ventilator-Free Breathing (PVFB) Trials**:\n- Although C3 ASIA A patients rarely achieve full ventilator independence, some patients develop accessory muscle breathing capacity sufficient for brief periods off the ventilator\n- **Prerequisites**: Medically stable, afebrile, no acute pulmonary issues, FiO2 ≤0.40, PEEP ≤5\n- **Protocol**:\n  - Start with **tracheostomy collar trials** (humidified O2 via trach collar, ventilator disconnected)\n  - Initial trial: 1-5 minutes with continuous SpO2 and ETCO2 monitoring\n  - Abort criteria: SpO2 <92%, HR >120 or <50, RR >35, patient distress, PaCO2 >50\n  - If tolerated: Increase by 5-minute increments per session, twice daily\n  - Goal: Maximize ventilator-free time for quality of life (speech, eating, mobility) even if full weaning is not achievable\n  - **Realistic expectation for C3 ASIA A**: May achieve 15-60 minutes of ventilator-free breathing using accessory muscles; 24-hour ventilator independence is unlikely without diaphragm pacing\n\n**2. Tracheostomy Management**:\n- **Cuff management**: Deflate cuff during the day when possible to allow:\n  - Above-cuff airflow for **speech** (one-way speaking valve -- Passy-Muir valve)\n  - Improved swallowing function\n  - Reduced tracheal mucosal damage\n- **Passy-Muir Valve (PMV)**: Trial with cuff fully deflated; requires adequate airway around the trach tube\n  - Benefits: Voice production, improved swallowing, better secretion management, psychological benefit\n  - Monitor: SpO2, work of breathing, patient comfort\n- **Tracheostomy downsizing**: Consider downsizing from #8 to #6 Shiley if ventilation is adequate (allows more airflow around tube)\n- **Trach site care**: Daily cleaning, inner cannula changes, monitor for granulation tissue\n\n**3. Secretion Management and Pneumonia Prevention**:\n- **Mechanical insufflation-exsufflation (MI-E / CoughAssist)**: 4-6 sessions daily; delivers positive pressure insufflation followed by rapid negative pressure exsufflation to simulate a cough\n  - Settings: +40/-40 cmH2O, 3-5 cycles per treatment\n  - **Single most important device for secretion clearance** in high SCI\n- **Suctioning**: PRN via tracheostomy; gentle technique to minimize mucosal trauma; pre-oxygenate before suctioning\n- **Chest physiotherapy**: Manual percussion and vibration; postural drainage (modified for ventilator patient)\n- **Nebulizers**: Albuterol Q4-6H for bronchodilation; normal saline nebulizers to thin secretions\n- **Incentive spirometry**: Not applicable (no voluntary breathing effort)\n- **Pneumonia prevention bundle**:\n  - Head of bed elevation ≥30 degrees\n  - Oral care with chlorhexidine BID\n  - Aspiration precautions (swallow evaluation before any oral intake)\n  - Flu and pneumococcal vaccination\n  - Hand hygiene and respiratory precautions for all staff/visitors\n\n**4. Nutritional Support**:\n- PEG tube feeds for primary nutrition (swallowing evaluation pending)\n- High-protein formula to support respiratory muscle maintenance and prevent catabolism\n- Monitor for aspiration; advance to oral diet only after formal swallow study with SLP\n\n**5. Additional Pulmonary Considerations**:\n- **Glossopharyngeal breathing (GPB/frog breathing)**: Teach this technique -- patient uses tongue and pharyngeal muscles to gulp air into lungs; can provide emergency ventilation if ventilator disconnects; provides psychological sense of control\n- **Abdominal binder**: Improves diaphragm mechanics if any residual function is present; also helps with venous return and orthostatic management\n- **Respiratory muscle training**: If any accessory muscle function returns, progressive strengthening with resistive devices\n- **Pulmonary function monitoring**: Serial FVC, NIF (negative inspiratory force), and peak cough flow measurements',
    ),
    Section(
