    Section(
      title: '+ Compare discharge disposition options for a ventilator-dependent SCI patient and outline the requirements for safe home ventilator discharge.',
      content:
          '**Discharge Disposition Options**:\n\n**Option 1: Long-Term Acute Care (LTAC)**:\n- Appropriate for: Patients still requiring active medical management, ongoing ventilator weaning attempts, or treatment of complications\n- Pros: 24/7 nursing with ventilator expertise; respiratory therapist on-site; physician availability\n- Cons: Institutional setting; limited rehabilitation services; not a long-term solution; insurance coverage typically limited (average stay 25-30 days)\n- Role: Transitional step if patient is not yet stable enough for home or SNF\n\n**Option 2: Skilled Nursing Facility (Ventilator Unit)**:\n- Appropriate for: Patients who are medically stable but cannot be safely managed at home\n- Pros: 24/7 nursing care; ventilator management; structured environment\n- Cons: Institutional living (devastating for a 22-year-old); limited autonomy; high infection risk; high depression rates; very limited community access; **significantly worse quality of life outcomes** compared to home discharge\n- Cost: \$200,000-\$400,000+ per year\n\n**Option 3: Home Ventilator Program** (PREFERRED if feasible):\n- Appropriate for: Medically stable, motivated patient with adequate caregiver support and funding\n- Pros: Best quality of life outcomes; greatest autonomy; lowest infection rates; ability to return to school/work; family proximity\n- Cons: Requires extensive caregiver training, home modification, equipment, and 24/7 skilled attendant care; high upfront costs; caregiver burnout risk\n- Cost: \$150,000-\$300,000+ per year (but often less than institutional care)\n\n**Requirements for Safe Home Ventilator Discharge**:\n\n**1. Equipment**:\n- **Two ventilators** (primary and backup -- mandatory for life-sustaining ventilation)\n- **Backup generator** (power failure plan)\n- **Portable suction machine** (battery-operated)\n- **CoughAssist device (MI-E)**\n- **Pulse oximeter with alarms**\n- **Manual resuscitation bag (Ambu bag)** at bedside at all times\n- **Tracheostomy supplies**: replacement trach tubes (same size and one size smaller), ties, suction catheters, saline\n- **Hospital bed** with pressure redistribution mattress\n- **Power wheelchair** with ventilator tray, head/chin control\n- **Patient lift system** (ceiling track or portable Hoyer)\n- **Environmental control unit (ECU)**: Allows patient to control lights, TV, phone, computer, door locks via head/mouth controls\n\n**2. Caregiver Training** (minimum 2-3 weeks in-hospital training):\n- Trained caregivers must be available **24/7** (minimum 2 trained caregivers)\n- Training includes: ventilator operation and troubleshooting, tracheostomy care and emergency change, suctioning, CoughAssist use, emergency manual ventilation (Ambu bag), skin care and turning, bowel/bladder program, medication administration, recognizing autonomic dysreflexia\n- Competency must be documented and assessed before discharge\n\n**3. Home Modifications**:\n- Wheelchair-accessible entrance (ramp), doorways (36+ inches), bathroom\n- Adequate electrical capacity for ventilator, suction, and medical equipment\n- Backup power source (generator or battery backup)\n- Notify local power company and fire department of ventilator-dependent resident\n\n**4. Insurance and Funding**:\n- Private insurance, Medicaid waiver programs (home and community-based services), or combination\n- Prior authorization for: home ventilator, backup ventilator, nursing hours (typically 16-24 hours/day of skilled nursing), respiratory therapy visits, DME\n- Medicaid HCBS waiver may cover attendant care services that allow home living\n- Vocational rehabilitation referral for return-to-school support (assistive technology, note-takers, accommodations)\n\n**5. Emergency Plan**:\n- Written emergency protocol posted at bedside and on refrigerator\n- Local EMS notified of ventilator-dependent patient at address\n- Emergency contact chain (caregiver, family, physician, 911)\n- Regular emergency drills with caregivers (ventilator failure, trach decannulation, power outage)',
    ),
    Section(
      title: 'DOMAIN E: INTERPERSONAL AND COMMUNICATION SKILLS',
      content:
          'During a family meeting, the patient\'s parents and the rehabilitation team are discussing goals of care. The mother states emphatically: "We want everything done. Every treatment, every surgery, every option -- we want him to have the best chance." Meanwhile, the night nurse privately reports that the patient has been mouthing "let me go" and "I don\'t want to live like this" on two occasions when family is not present.',
