    Section(
      title: '+ Navigate this ethical dilemma involving patient autonomy, surrogate decision-making, and end-of-life wishes in a young ventilator-dependent patient.',
      content:
          '**Ethical Framework and Approach**:\n\nThis is a conflict between **patient autonomy** (the patient\'s own expressed wishes) and **surrogate/family wishes**. The foundational bioethical principle is that a **competent patient\'s wishes take precedence** over family wishes, even when the patient is ventilator-dependent.\n\n**Step 1: Assess Decision-Making Capacity**:\nBefore acting on either the family\'s or patient\'s statements, I must formally assess the patient\'s decision-making capacity:\n- Does he understand his medical condition and prognosis?\n- Does he understand the consequences of withdrawing ventilatory support (death)?\n- Can he reason about the decision and weigh risks/benefits?\n- Is his decision consistent over time (not just a momentary expression of distress)?\n- Is he free from undue influence, delirium, or untreated depression?\n\n**Critical consideration**: This patient is 22 years old and only 3 weeks post-injury. **Acute adjustment disorder and depression are extremely common** in early SCI and can profoundly affect decision-making. His statements may reflect transient despair rather than a settled, informed wish to die.\n\n**Step 2: Private Conversation with the Patient**:\n"I want to talk to you about something important, just the two of us. The nurses have shared with me that you have expressed some thoughts about not wanting to continue living. I take that very seriously, and I want to understand what you are feeling.\n\nCan you help me understand what you mean when you say you want to let go? Are you in pain? Are you afraid of what your life will look like? Are you feeling hopeless right now?\n\nI am asking because I want to help you, not because I am going to dismiss what you are saying. Your feelings are valid. At the same time, I want to make sure you have all the information you need and that we are addressing any suffering that we can address.\n\nIt is also important for you to know that it has only been 3 weeks. Many people with injuries like yours have told me they felt exactly the way you feel right now, and that those feelings changed significantly over time -- especially once they were out of the hospital, back in their community, and connected to others living with similar injuries.\n\nI am not going to make any promises about what your life will look like. But I will promise you this: I will be honest with you, I will treat your pain and suffering, and I will respect your decisions. I would like to connect you with a psychologist who specializes in adjustment after spinal cord injury, and also with a peer mentor -- someone close to your age who has a high cervical injury and is living in the community."\n\n**Step 3: Address Family Separately**:\n"I understand how much you love your son and want to fight for him. That is exactly what a parent should do. I need to share with you that he has expressed some distress and ambivalence about his situation. This is very common in early spinal cord injury and does not necessarily mean he has made a decision about his care.\n\nWhat I want to do is make sure we are treating any depression and giving him time, support, and information before any major decisions are made. I also want to involve our ethics team and a psychologist.\n\nI need you to understand something important: if your son is competent to make decisions, his wishes ultimately guide his care, even if they differ from yours. That is his legal and ethical right. But right now, we are not at a point where anyone needs to make that decision. We are at a point where we need to support him, treat his suffering, and give him time."\n\n**Step 4: Action Plan**:\n1. **Psychiatry consultation**: Formal capacity assessment and evaluation for major depression, adjustment disorder, and acute suicidality\n2. **Psychology referral**: Begin regular sessions focused on coping, grief processing, and adjustment\n3. **Peer mentoring**: Connect patient with a high-cervical SCI peer who lives in the community (this is one of the most powerful interventions for early SCI despair)\n4. **Ethics committee consultation**: Document the situation and engage the institutional ethics committee proactively\n5. **Palliative care consultation**: Not for end-of-life planning necessarily, but for holistic symptom management, goals-of-care conversations, and supporting the patient and family through this process\n6. **Time**: Do not rush any decisions. Research shows that most SCI patients who express early desire to die do NOT maintain that wish long-term when adequately supported. Longitudinal studies show quality of life in high cervical SCI is rated surprisingly high by patients living in the community.\n7. **Documentation**: Document all conversations, capacity assessments, and the patient\'s expressed wishes carefully in the medical record\n8. **Reassess regularly**: Revisit goals of care at regular intervals as the patient stabilizes emotionally and progresses through rehabilitation',
    ),
  ],
